{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/node.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:16:47.310770Z","id":"a0937fc2020a2f554080a1b7345cae32c4ac91accde3c4d02c6dbbae29446a0a","body":{"detail":"Failure: in a five-voter Cluster (ids 1..5, fresh state), node 1 wins pre-votes from 3 and 4, campaigns in term 1, and only node 3 receives its Vote request. Node 3 grants, and that Voted{term:1,granted:true} is delayed. Node 2 then pre-votes and campaigns in term 1 with nodes 4 and 5 and becomes Leader of term 1 (2,4,5 voted for it). Node 1 times out while still Candidate. tick() (node.rs:360-375) sets prevoting=Some(2), clears `votes` to {1}, and leaves `role == Role::Candidate` and hard.term == 1. Node 4 has not yet heard an Append from node 2 and grants PreVoted{campaign:2}, so node 1's votes become {1,4}. The delayed Voted{term:1,granted:true} from node 3 then arrives. step() treats it as current-term (node.rs:449 does not reject it), and the Voted arm (node.rs:487-493) only checks `self.role == Role::Candidate`. It sets votes[3]=true, election_quorum() sees {1,3,4}, and become_leader() runs in term 1. Result: nodes 1 and 2 are both Leader in term 1. Each appends its own barrier (index 1, term 1), so one LogId names entries from two leaders, which violates Election Safety and Log Matching (Raft paper Figure 3, https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf). I reproduced this with the public Cluster API (tick/step/ready/next_message) in a throwaway binary outside the repository: final output `A role Leader term 1; B role Leader term 1`. The root cause is that pre-vote grants (which are for term 2 and are not durable votes) and real term-1 votes are counted in the same `votes` array. The Voted handler cannot tell which kind it is counting while `prevoting.is_some()`. The fixed `Node` (hooks None) is not affected because its tick() calls campaign() directly.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":360},"end":{"line":375},"content_hash":"eb68a0b2536c8898ae3945a2fce584343851b3c81eb5a08f1e93a13c364860f5"},"suggested_fix":"When starting a pre-vote in tick(), step down to a non-candidate state (set `self.role = Role::Follower`, as the Raft thesis pre-candidate does), or guard the Voted arm with `self.prevoting.is_none()`. Better still, track pre-vote grants in a separate array from real votes. Add a regression test with the five-node schedule above that asserts at most one leader per term.","summary":"A Candidate that starts a pre-vote keeps role Candidate and shares `votes` with the probe, so a late same-term Voted grant plus pre-vote grants elect it leader of the old term, which breaks election safety","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/node.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:25:07.514013Z","id":"2773c2689bad46afd14ed5a54e54f7a71f2e4b5e9458ef436bfe7cb33ac237d3","body":{"detail":"At HEAD jarl/src/node.rs: tick() with hooks (360-375) sets prevoting=Some(term+1), resets votes to {self}, and does not change role or hard.term. step() records PreVoted grants into the same votes array (441-442). A same-term Voted passes the stale check (449), and the Voted arm (487-493) checks only role == Candidate before setting votes[peer] and calling become_leader(). PreVote grant (413-418) needs only term > hard.term, role != Leader, and leader_age >= election_ticks; leader_age starts at u64::MAX (150), so a follower that has voted but not yet heard an Append still grants. Reproduced against jarl at the HEAD source (git diff HEAD -- jarl/src is empty) with a standalone binary that uses Cluster<u8,u8,5,16> through tick/step/ready/next_message only, ids 1..5, Settings{heartbeat 2, election 10}. Trace: 1->3,1->4 PreVote(1) granted; node1 Candidate term 1; 1->3 Vote(1), reply 3->1 Voted(1,true) held; node 2 pre-votes and votes with 4,5 and becomes Leader term 1; node1 ticks to timeout and stays Candidate term 1; 1->4 PreVote(2) -> 4->1 PreVoted(t1,c2,true); held 3->1 Voted(1,true) delivered. Final output: `A role Leader term 1; B role Leader term 1`. Two leaders in one term violate Election Safety, so blocker is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"a0937fc2020a2f554080a1b7345cae32c4ac91accde3c4d02c6dbbae29446a0a","summary":"confirmed: a Candidate that starts a pre-vote keeps role Candidate, and a late term-1 Voted grant plus pre-vote grants elect a second term-1 leader","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/cluster.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:34:54.041097Z","id":"c88aefc2060fcefdaa8f76e41831aca06a393826b317941f61186ecb0efef891","body":{"detail":"cluster.rs:274 says 'Poll `membership` and call `finish_reconfiguration` after the joint record commits.' `Cluster::membership` (cluster.rs:153-156) returns `node.membership`, which `refresh_membership` (node.rs:817-818) sets from the latest appended record. Cluster.rs:93 also says configuration records 'become effective when appended, before commitment'. So `membership().is_joint()` is true as soon as `reconfigure` returns. A host that follows the doc calls `finish_reconfiguration` too early and gets `Error::Reconfiguring` (cluster.rs:304-306). The accessor that reports the commit is `committed_membership()` (cluster.rs:157-160). README.md:89 has the same gap: it says 'Once the joint entry commits' but names no way to observe that.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":271},"end":{"line":275},"content_hash":"1b6a7f12746d4c06e5a73343bbc5a8dae47b536e93c5ce7695991ac59f0d1533"},"suggested_fix":"Change cluster.rs:274 to 'Poll `committed_membership().is_joint()`', or add a `Cluster::reconfiguration_committed()` predicate. In README.md step 4, name the same accessor.","summary":"reconfigure rustdoc says to poll `membership` for the joint commit, but `membership()` turns joint at append time, not at commit","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/cluster.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:34:54.041158Z","id":"8182b9cf330f0b8c0498eae927ab7f2bab6429f68ee7feaf58287e46602c535c","body":{"detail":"`membership_at(last)` (node.rs:802-815) returns `(index, m)`, where `index` is the position of the newest configuration record or snapshot. The clause `index > hard.commit` already rejects every case where that record is uncommitted. When `index <= hard.commit`, no configuration record lies between `index` and `commit`, so `membership_at(hard.commit)` returns the same record and `!= membership` is always false. The dead clause suggests a second invariant that is not there. It also uses the derived array `PartialEq` on `Membership`, which compares slot layout and not member sets (see membership.rs:16-19). A future edit that rebuilds either value would then fail for reasons that have nothing to do with configuration.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":230},"end":{"line":237},"content_hash":"17afa84036557bc9cbb9ee30366795a10cc2483b62b1d83aa977bbeb15d86680"},"suggested_fix":"Delete `|| self.node.membership_at(hard.commit).1 != membership`. Alternatively, keep it as a `debug_assert_eq!` with a comment saying it is implied by `index <= hard.commit`.","summary":"The third Reconfiguring clause in change_available can never fire, and the comparison it makes depends on slot order","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/membership.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:34:54.041196Z","id":"dc5b991d85b0618e298aa71708e63a6557bf6e03970e95242d13d45edf4fc626","body":{"detail":"`Membership` derives `Eq, PartialEq` over `members: [Option<Member>; MAX]`. `include` (membership.rs:47-66) puts each identity in the first free slot in argument order. So `Membership::<3>::new(&[Id(1), Id(2)], &[]) != Membership::<3>::new(&[Id(2), Id(1)], &[])`, although both describe the same configuration, and `finalized()` (membership.rs:133-143) can leave holes with `None`. Equality is part of the public API, and `Record`/`Checkpoint` derive `PartialEq` on top of it. A host that compares `cluster.membership()` with a configuration it decoded or built itself, as an administrator's 'is the change done?' check, can get a false negative whenever the encoder or the caller orders identities differently. Jarl's own `change_available` (cluster.rs:234) also uses this comparison.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":16},"end":{"line":19},"content_hash":"91ed16c7cdb0690eb5f7e374b17236032fb61f6ea28d609625a29b14954a7931"},"suggested_fix":"Implement `PartialEq` by hand, comparing the sets {voters}, {old_voters} and {learners} regardless of slot order. Alternatively, canonicalize slot order in `restore`, `with_learners`, `joint` and `finalized` (for example, sort by `Id`) and document that equality is structural.","summary":"Derived PartialEq on public Membership compares slot layout, so equal voter/learner sets given in a different order compare unequal","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:34:54.041233Z","id":"45b8d35f1383d55d27b374ba7844b6332d037962c178df911d36454650c74526","body":{"detail":"`Error::Config` is documented as 'Invalid membership, timing, or zero log capacity', but `Cluster::new` returns it for any `CAP < 4` (cluster.rs:104-106, not stated in that function's rustdoc), and `propose_batch(&[])` returns it via `propose_many` (node.rs:598-600). `Error::Full` is documented as 'Log capacity is exhausted. Compact applied entries or increase capacity', but `reconfigure` also returns it when the old/new peer union exceeds `MAX` (membership.rs:129), and `set_learners` returns it when voters plus learners exceed `MAX` (cluster.rs:261-263); compacting or growing `CAP` cannot fix either. `State::restore` returns `Full` for a checkpoint with more entries than `CAP` (state.rs:69), where its doc promises rejection of inconsistent metadata. `Error::Reconfiguring` is documented as 'A membership change must finish committing', but a newly elected leader with no pending change also gets it until an entry from its own term commits (cluster.rs:238-252). In each case a host that branches on the variant takes the wrong recovery action.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":97},"end":{"line":120},"content_hash":"d2bf08126f97ad00a7f127876a5aa3cc1e6bd49644523b08068ff9c66a9757a2"},"suggested_fix":"Widen the variant docs: Config 'or `CAP < 4` for Cluster, or an empty batch'; Full 'log or peer (`MAX`) capacity'; Reconfiguring 'or leadership not yet established by a commit in this term'. Alternatively, add distinct variants (for example `PeerCapacity`, `NotReady`). Also document `CAP >= 4` on `Cluster::new` and the `Full` case on `State::restore`.","summary":"Error variant docs do not match several places that return them in Cluster and State","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/cluster.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:34:54.041261Z","id":"65823bdd12c3e85920278d07e78a4f82a8042fc4974d25944fd7d331c0a38ff2","body":{"detail":"`reconfigure` rejects with `NotCaughtUp` unless every promoted learner has `matched(id) >= state().last().index` (cluster.rs:283). `last` includes every proposal admitted since the learner's last acknowledgment, and `change_available` requires an idle node (no pending save or outbox), so any proposal accepted since the last reply makes the check fail. The Raft dissertation (Ongaro 2014, section 4.2.1, https://github.com/ongardie/dissertation) catches up new servers in rounds and accepts once a round finishes within an election timeout, because exact equality is not needed for safety: joint consensus is safe however far the learner lags, and the catch-up rule is only for availability. With the current rule a busy host must pause application proposals to promote anyone. Neither the rustdoc nor README.md:84-85 says so.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":281},"end":{"line":287},"content_hash":"f74130eb50d88b06119c5ee40b789ce71a35912909d7aad94ac9a0fe5f4311ee"},"suggested_fix":"Either document that the host must quiesce proposals until `matched(id) == state().last().index` before calling `reconfigure`, or relax the check to a bounded lag such as `last - matched <= MAX_APPEND_ENTRIES`, or 'within the committed index', and state the availability rationale.","summary":"Promotion requires matched == last log index at the instant of the call, which a leader under steady proposal load may rarely satisfy","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/ready.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:34:54.041288Z","id":"092869008fd0705d9c8856975a26045a1db512625f764121785d7f03dd3f9022","body":{"detail":"`State::write` (state.rs:101-113) computes `entries` as the slice starting at `truncate_from - base - 1`, and leaves it empty when `truncate_from` is `None`. So a nonempty `entries()` always has `truncate_from == Some(first.id.index)`. Example journals (examples/support/dynamic_journal.rs:83-84) and tests (tests/async_host.rs:36, tests/simulation.rs:46) depend on this, but ready.rs:3-10 does not state it. Because of the same structure, saving one `Write` twice is harmless: truncate, then append the same suffix. Replaying an older `Write` after a newer one truncates the newer entries. That is the hazard host.rs:21-23 and README.md:122-123 warn about without explaining. Backend authors need both facts to design a journal or a cancellation-safe async worker.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":3},"end":{"line":19},"content_hash":"1081857efa4d5b77d92a0ce8382092cfedb810a6179d36ad61d8b1174596c035"},"suggested_fix":"Add to the `Write` rustdoc: 'If `entries()` is nonempty, `truncate_from` is `Some` and equals the first entry's index. Applying the same Write twice is idempotent; applying an earlier Write after a later one can discard committed-by-quorum entries.' Link to it from `AsyncStorage`.","summary":"Write rustdoc omits the invariants hosts rely on: entries begin exactly at truncate_from, and replaying the same Write is idempotent but replaying an older one is not","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/cluster.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:37:01.411019Z","id":"19b570d87e7fcb35567cc9062d506e65aa85a43d214cde5ee8dce11263b9ad2d","body":{"detail":"jarl/src/cluster.rs:273-274 says 'Poll `membership` and call `finish_reconfiguration` after the joint record commits'. cluster.rs:154-156 returns node.membership (latest appended config); cluster.rs:158-160 committed_membership() is the commit-position view; cluster.rs:304-306 returns Reconfiguring while the joint record is above commit. A scratch program depending on jarl by path (3 voters + caught-up learner Id(3), reconfigure to {0,1,2,3}, persist on the leader only, before delivering appends) printed: `reconfigure: Ok(LogId { index: 4, term: 1 })`, `membership().is_joint()=true committed_membership().is_joint()=false`, `finish_reconfiguration before commit: Err(Reconfiguring)`. README.md:89 ('Once the joint entry commits') names no accessor either.","kind":"comment","references":"c88aefc2060fcefdaa8f76e41831aca06a393826b317941f61186ecb0efef891","summary":"confirmed: membership() turns joint at append, so polling it cannot detect the joint commit","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/cluster.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:37:01.411059Z","id":"17eabdab04dcc5e92f3dd3dc7608a8e8a6463f94345314803f51c06ee9abe15b","body":{"detail":"jarl/src/cluster.rs:231-234: `(index, membership) = membership_at(last)`. node.rs:806-818 membership_at returns the newest config record (or snapshot/bootstrap) at or below the argument. If cluster.rs:233 `index > hard.commit` is false, no configuration record lies in (index, last] and hence none in (index, commit], and the snapshot base is <= commit, so membership_at(hard.commit) yields the same (index, value) pair; the third clause cannot be true. The comparison uses derived PartialEq on Membership (membership.rs:16), which is slot-order sensitive (scratch program: `Membership::<3>::new(&[Id(1), Id(2)]) == new(&[Id(2), Id(1)])` prints false), though here both sides come from the same record.","kind":"comment","references":"8182b9cf330f0b8c0498eae927ab7f2bab6429f68ee7feaf58287e46602c535c","summary":"confirmed: the membership_at(hard.commit) != membership clause is implied false by index <= hard.commit","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/membership.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:37:01.411069Z","id":"11e98874723b0e4d68e695a58c27c7443ce38f429acf4f289f5b04477caa7896","body":{"detail":"jarl/src/membership.rs:16-19 derives Eq/PartialEq over `members: [Option<Member>; MAX]`; include (membership.rs:47-66) fills the first free slot in argument order; finalized (membership.rs:133-143) leaves None holes. Scratch program depending on jarl by path printed `[eq] new([1,2]) == new([2,1]): false` for `Membership::<3>::new(&[Id(1), Id(2)], &[])` vs `Membership::<3>::new(&[Id(2), Id(1)], &[])`.","kind":"comment","references":"dc5b991d85b0618e298aa71708e63a6557bf6e03970e95242d13d45edf4fc626","summary":"confirmed: derived PartialEq compares slot layout; same voter set in different order compares unequal","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:37:01.411076Z","id":"a8aa1ab8402348dd3591eab515b686a19e955c71a2fe83953dd788d03daf91f3","body":{"detail":"Docs at jarl/src/lib.rs:100-117. Config: cluster.rs:104-106 returns it for CAP < 4 (doc says 'zero log capacity'); node.rs:602-603 returns it for an empty batch. Full: membership.rs:129 maps an old/new union overflow of MAX to Full; cluster.rs:261-262 returns Full when voters+learners > MAX; state.rs:153 (push, used by State::restore) returns Full when entries exceed CAP; none is fixed by compaction. Reconfiguring: cluster.rs:250-251 returns it when no entry of the current term is committed, with no change pending. Scratch program depending on jarl by path printed `[err] Cluster CAP=3 new: Some(Config)` and `[err] propose_batch(&[]): Err(Config)`.","kind":"comment","references":"45b8d35f1383d55d27b374ba7844b6332d037962c178df911d36454650c74526","summary":"confirmed: Config/Full/Reconfiguring are returned in cases their variant docs do not describe","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/cluster.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:37:01.411086Z","id":"684820f239a5a67a98c28fad28f33f1e62ea681d761fe9b61923c383e27e499d","body":{"detail":"jarl/src/cluster.rs:281-285 requires matched(id) >= state().last().index for each promoted learner. Scratch program (3 voters, learner Id(3) fully caught up: `matched(3)=2 last=2 commit=2`) then proposed one command, persisted and drained the leader's outbox (so change_available's idle check passes) and called reconfigure before delivery: `propose: Ok(LogId { index: 3, term: 1 })`, `reconfigure after one proposal: Err(NotCaughtUp) (matched(3)=2 last=3)`. After delivery the same call succeeded. README.md:84-85 states the catch-up target but not that proposals must pause.","kind":"comment","references":"65823bdd12c3e85920278d07e78a4f82a8042fc4974d25944fd7d331c0a38ff2","summary":"confirmed: any proposal admitted since the learner's last ack makes reconfigure fail with NotCaughtUp","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"jarl/src/ready.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T12:37:01.411094Z","id":"f2632aa33f4d4fced16c1593155e7200aa62add7e40e80ac77ff20f7bc5ad594","body":{"detail":"jarl/src/state.rs:101-113: offset = len when `from` is None (empty entries), else `from - base - 1`, so the first entry index is `from`. All log_from sources (node.rs:557 snapshot.last+1, node.rs:639, node.rs:914, node.rs:963-965) are > base. Hosts rely on it: examples/support/dynamic_journal.rs:83-84 encodes truncate_from; tests/reconfiguration.rs save() keeps disk entries below truncate_from then appends write.entries(). The rustdoc at jarl/src/ready.rs:3-25 gives apply order but neither the alignment invariant nor replay semantics; host.rs:21-22 only warns against older writes overtaking later ones.","kind":"comment","references":"092869008fd0705d9c8856975a26045a1db512625f764121785d7f03dd3f9022","summary":"confirmed: Write's entries start at truncate_from and are empty when it is None, but ready.rs does not state it","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
